sys_resolver
============

Zainium OS has no /usr, /bin or /lib at the root: the system lives under
/overlayer. Most software still asks for the classic paths. sys_resolver
makes those paths work, with no mount, no symlink, no kernel patch and no
change to the programs themselves.

It is a library preloaded into programs, libsys_resolver.so, and a small
tool, sys-resolver. A path that exists is never touched. When a lookup
fails with ENOENT, the path is tried again under the real roots, in order:

    /overlayer/syshub
    /overlayer/syshub/x86_64-zainium-linux-musl
    /overlayer/zexlib/union
    /overlayer/zaisys

So /usr/bin/env opens /overlayer/syshub/bin/env. Only paths starting with
bin, sbin, lib, usr, opt, var, etc or boot are resolved; /home and /tmp
are left alone.

What it does
------------

  - resolves paths in file calls: open, stat, access, opendir, readlink,
    realpath, dlopen and the rest, for musl and glibc programs
  - starts scripts whose #! interpreter is not at the path they name
    (#!/bin/sh, #!/usr/bin/env python3), however they are run
  - starts prebuilt programs whose loader is missing, such as
    /lib64/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2, through this system's copy of it
  - refuses to delete files of the base, for programs other than zex. This
    is a safety net against a stray rm -rf, not a security boundary

The library has no libc of its own. Its calls bind to the libc of the
program it is loaded into, so a glibc program never gets musl loaded next
to glibc.

The tool
--------

    sys-resolver resolve <path>    what a classic path resolves to now
    sys-resolver doctor            audit the resolver against the system
    sys-resolver get               which libc builds use, and why

Building for glibc
------------------

Plain gcc builds for musl. To build for glibc (the glibc and
glibc-toolchain packages):

    eval "$(sys-resolver env glibc)"     for one shell or a ZEXBUILD
    elevate sys-resolver set glibc       for the whole system

"env musl" and "set musl" go back. ZAINIUM_LIBC=musl|glibc overrides the
setting for one environment. Only building changes: every program keeps
running with the libc it was linked against.

Not covered
-----------

  - lookups libc makes internally, such as musl's timezone search
  - static and setuid programs: the loader preloads nothing into them
  - the loader of a program started by a static or setuid program

Building
--------

    cargo test --release
    ./build-so.sh

build-so.sh writes target/libsys_resolver.so. Use
CC=x86_64-zainium-linux-musl-gcc on Zainium OS. The library is installed
as /overlayer/syshub/lib/libsys_resolver.so and preloaded through
LD_PRELOAD in the system environment.

